WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:06.970
Hi, I'm Nick Gillespie with Reason TV and today
we're talking with John Mackey. He's the

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:11.140
co-founder and co-CEO of Whole Foods
market. John, thanks for talking to us.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:12.740
Thanks for having me on, Nick.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:17.140
This is a point in time where we have a presidential election between the two most

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:25.440
disliked and statist politicians in a generation at least. The economy is
slow, but is also kind of inventive.

00:00:25.449 --> 00:00:30.369
You are a very high-profile libertarian. Is
this a good time to be a libertarian or

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:33.700
a bad time to be a libertarian? It's a
bad time to be a libertarian in the

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:41.530
sense that the left appears to be
increasingly trying to police correct

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:47.500
thinking, correct speech, and they
tend to go after those who do not you

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:53.859
know whether it be on climate change or
on gay marriage...it's a real chilling

00:00:53.859 --> 00:00:58.179
effect for any kind of freedom of speech
and in that sense it's a bad time. It's a good

00:00:58.179 --> 00:01:01.749
time in the sense that who knows what's
going to happen to the Republican Party.

00:01:01.749 --> 00:01:08.890
They could suffer a melt down in this election and if that happens then possible

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:13.180
change within that party might be
possible...serious change for the first

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:14.740
time in a long, long time.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.780
That could be a libertarian opportunity.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:23.079
You were a supporter of Rand Paul in the election or in the Republican primary system

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:25.880
what do you think went wrong with his
candidacy?

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:32.020
I think several factors came into play. One is he was not seen... unlike his father who was seen as an

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:37.719
outsider...I don't think Rand was seen as
an outsider any longer. He was a senator.

00:01:37.719 --> 00:01:44.680
He didn't...he thought he would get his
father's support and he didn't get it.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:49.960
So I think that was the biggest
factor and then you had this...at a time

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:56.229
when terrorism is increasing the
message that we need to be less

00:01:56.229 --> 00:02:01.420
aggressive militarily doesn't...is not
playing that well in mainstream

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:06.820
America who's scared. So that message did not land well. The security message was a

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:12.400
problem. And then I don't think his
economic message was...which I agreed with...

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.200
but I just don't think people are as that
interested in that right now.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.140
So I think his timing might have been bad.
And then you have the whole populist

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:25.200
thing with the nativism movement that
Trump has capitalized on so much.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:30.540
I think he was the outsider candidate and Rand Paul is the insider and just

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:35.140
one of many insiders and was rejected by
the electorate.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:41.840
What is your thought on Trump? Is he...you know...is he
plausible in any way for you as a

00:02:41.849 --> 00:02:44.889
candidate or as a president?

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:47.940
Uh, no. He's not.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:51.620
You're a successful businessman. He is a successful businessman.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:55.470
What do you sense is his kind of
philosophy of business or of commerce or of

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:58.019
economics and how does it differ from
yours?

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:03.360
I would say first of all he seems to me
to be almost a prototypical crony capitalist.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.220
Meaning he's made donations to
both sides of the political party.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:12.260
Oftentimes in real estate you need to
get certain approvals through various

00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:16.410
government agencies and you got to be... you got to play both sides of the fence to

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:23.190
get deals done. He's a...I think Trump sees
himself primarily as a great deal maker.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:29.250
And he wrote a book on it..."The Art of the Deal"...so he sees politics through that

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:37.769
spectrum, through that lens. And for him I
think he doesn't fit the kind of

00:03:37.769 --> 00:03:43.170
entrepreneurial capitalist that I
feel like that I represent.

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:49.109
Non-cronyism, creating value for other people. And one of the interesting things of

00:03:49.109 --> 00:03:54.000
course is that he is seen as a
capitalist. He's seen as a businessman.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.340
And yet, in a lot of ways, he's not
really that well...a good representative of it.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:03.239
Talk about Hillary Clinton because these are the choices, right? One of

00:04:03.239 --> 00:04:06.840
them almost certainly is going to be the next President of the United States.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.700
What do you worry about under a Hillary
Clinton presidency?

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:15.120
I mean there seems to be compelling evidence of the Clintons not being...

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:21.190
they're certainly not straight shooters. They seem to...Hillary seems to be...

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:25.450
as much as I complain about Trump being a crony capitalist, I think Hillary falls

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:29.770
into that category herself. I mean you
just look at the money she received from

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:34.600
all the speeches she's made and it's
like a who's who of the financial sector,

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:41.169
health care, and defense contractors. It's
astounding. And then you see the Clinton

00:04:41.169 --> 00:04:45.970
Initiative with a lot of money pouring
in from foreign governments...

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:51.280
it's disturbing. I mean that doesn't...that's a
smoking gun it doesn't necessarily mean

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:56.410
that any bullets were fired, but it is
concerning to me to say the least.

00:04:56.410 --> 00:05:02.560
Plus, we have the record of the Clinton
administration in the past

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.800
and you had all those pardons
granted at the end. It's...

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:07.780
it's disturbing. I worry about it.

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:09.780
But there is a third term out there at least..or a third

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:14.460
possibility which is Gary Johnson and Bill
Weld running as the Libertarian Party ticket.

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:15.150
What do you think of them?

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:20.210
I don't know Weld. I know Gary and yeah I like Gary.

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:22.800
Is he the perfect libertarian candidate?

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:29.140
No. But his overall...I probably agree with
him on about ninety percent of the stuff.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:35.289
And that's far higher than I agree with
Hillary or Trump. So the interesting

00:05:35.289 --> 00:05:38.710
thing there is they're polling pretty high.
But when people get in there to vote...

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:42.250
I mean I'm going to vote for Johnson so...
but when they get in there to vote

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:45.850
because the old argument...you're just throwing your vote away is such a powerful

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:50.080
argument that they're probably...he's
probably going to get fewer votes than

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:55.570
he's polling at the end of the day. But
still, I mean if the libertarians were

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:59.350
to get two or three percent that would
be an outstanding election cycle for

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:02.030
them because they've never gotten that
much before.

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:07.000
They're running in that kind of fat middle of where the country really is that people are

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:11.110
generally socially tolerant and fiscally
responsible. They want a government

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:16.450
that is less in your business and you
know and can pay its own bills.

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:20.410
Do you think that that kind of thinking is
actually ascendant? I mean clearly the

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:24.580
Republican and Democratic parties don't
give outlets to that kind of thinking, but

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:29.200
is America moving in that kind of
soft libertarian direction? I thought so when I read you

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:29.590
and Matt's

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:33.760
book a few years ago but it
doesn't seem to be playing out that way.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:41.590
I mean the nativism that I had no
idea until Trump ascended how powerful

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:45.760
the nativism sentiments are because I
think that's the key to understand Trump.

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:52.300
It's nativistic in immigration,
nativistic in jobs, in foreign policy,

00:06:52.300 --> 00:07:00.340
in taxes, and in every way it seems
very strong nativistic philosophy.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:04.510
One of the interesting things and it hasn't
really come into play yet,

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:09.400
but polls routinely show that even
fifty percent or more of Republicans

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:14.050
believe in not just in current levels of
immigration, but a pathway to citizenship

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:18.550
or legality for currently illegals. And
Democrats it's a little bit higher.

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:22.990
So it will be interesting to see if
nativism...it clearly carried Trump

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:26.800
through the primary and then what
happens in a general election? Your

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:30.160
business in many ways is built on kind
of free trade agreements. The fact that

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:34.540
you can bring in goods from all over the
world and put them into one place.

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:38.920
Why does it...you know that a lot of
people are okay with on the right, but

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:41.680
then when you talk about bringing it...
letting people from all over the world

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:42.820
move here...

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:46.660
what is the switch that is
flipped that makes that a totally different

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:48.760
and much more terrifying scenario?

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:51.160
If you study history the United States and I've

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:53.680
studied it very carefully for a long
time

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:59.230
always when we're doing immigration
there's...whenever there's been

00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:03.940
accelerated immigration in American history
there have been race riots and whether it be

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:11.770
the Irish coming in or Italians or there's
always been push back against it.

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:15.130
So it's like our nation can handle a certain
amount of immigration, but when it gets

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:21.070
to be too much too fast then the culture
is unable to absorb it effectively.

00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:26.410
Americanize it so to speak. And people
become scared and threatened by it. And I

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:29.980
think we're in one of those situations
right now. Plus I think there is an

00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:32.980
element of...probably element of racism
in it in the sense that

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:40.140
fear of non-whites and it's...

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:42.220
I think there's an element of that in it.

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:44.820
From your business perspective, what

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:47.580
are the biggest things holding back
economic growth? You know one of the

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:52.260
things over about the past 10 or 15 years...
average or economic growth has averaged

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:56.490
around two percent or less a year. The
historic average since World War II is

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:59.130
about three percent. So that's a big
variance.

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:01.440
It's a couple of obvious things.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:03.510
Most importantly is regulations.

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:07.500
I feel like there's almost a regulatory
war against corporations in America

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:11.250
right now. It's the number of
regulators attacking Whole Foods is

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:16.560
unprecedented in our entire history. And
honestly, I don't feel like...it's risky

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:20.339
for me to even talk about it because I
don't want additional regulatory

00:09:20.339 --> 00:09:25.710
harassment that cost our company tens of
millions of dollars. And I feel like

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:29.279
that's happening...I talked to other CEOs
and this is not just a Whole Foods

00:09:29.279 --> 00:09:33.600
problem. They're all feeling the
squeeze on a...regulatory

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:35.339
agencies harassing them.

00:09:35.339 --> 00:09:40.199
What's an example of a useless
regulation that you deal with that

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:44.960
you know it makes it harder to
deliver services and goods at a good price?

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:50.700
We're dealing with a toxic waste issue...
disposal of multiple vitamins that have

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:55.080
selenium in them. They have a little few micrograms and that's been classified by the EPA as

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:56.370
a toxic waste.

00:09:56.370 --> 00:09:57.510
What is selenium?

00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:00.839
Selenium is a trace mineral that we need nutritionally

00:10:00.839 --> 00:10:06.510
but only in very small little amounts. So
when we're...what do we do

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:09.360
when multiple vitamins are returned to
us for some reason they're

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:11.760
dissatisfied. We take it back.

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:16.320
Well, because it has selenium in it we can't
just throw that in the landfill of which

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:21.080
of course we did. We just throw it in the trash. That now has to be disposed of as a toxic waste.

00:10:21.089 --> 00:10:25.410
Well, I mean that sets up a whole toxic
waste disposal system for vitamins that

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:29.339
people are eating, okay? I would say
that seems like kind of a

00:10:29.339 --> 00:10:33.720
nonsensical regulation. But there's all
types of employment regulations. Very...

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:38.940
it's increasingly difficult in the United States to be able to terminate anyone,

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:42.750
particularly anyone that's in a protected class. The amount of

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:47.160
paperwork that you have to track and
even then you're probably still going to

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:50.760
get sued and very likely you'll either
lose you'll have to settle.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:53.910
To play devil's advocate people would
be like, "Well, it's good you shouldn't be

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:57.600
able to fire people quickly especially
people in protected classes because they're in

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:00.540
protected classes because they need
protection."

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:03.720
I disagree. I mean employment

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:08.900
is a voluntary exchange that occurs
between individuals who are looking for

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:14.280
work and employers are looking to hire. I
mean should people be able to quit

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:18.870
whenever they want to or should
we be able to force them to continue to

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:19.620
work for us?

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:26.070
I mean I firmly believe that it's not
in the best interest of any employer to

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:33.090
fire people arbitrarily. And this is
what's usually held up...abuse. And does

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:34.380
that occasionally occur?

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:37.860
Of course it does. That doesn't
necessarily mean that you have to set up

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:45.300
the system so that employers are
constantly being incented or harassed

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:50.040
if they do terminate someone. Because
again, why would they want to terminate a

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:51.089
good worker?

00:11:51.089 --> 00:11:55.680
They wouldn't. And but yet they're
constantly seen as guilty of doing

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.230
something wrong and then they have to
prove their innocence when they

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:01.230
terminate someone. But that doesn't go on
the other side of the equation.

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:07.230
If somebody terminates or resigns because
they found a better job or they

00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:11.130
don't like it or they're racist and they
don't like the company who knows.

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:13.170
But there's no...so it's to me a double
standard.

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:15.750
Talk about the 365 stores that

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:19.740
you're starting to roll out. How are
those different than a traditional Whole

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:23.420
Foods market and what are you hoping to
accomplish with them?

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:29.540
The stores are smaller than a traditional Whole Foods market. They have only about a quarter of

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:32.620
the other number of products that you
might have a Whole Foods. It's a very

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:36.680
curated product mix. And then we are
building these stores for about a third

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:41.030
of the cost of a regular Whole Foods
market. So the capital cost is

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:45.160
significantly lower. And then we've restructured

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:50.230
the stores to have far lower labor
component. The long and the short of it is

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:55.160
is that we've taken about a...what we call basis points a thousand basis points or ten

00:12:55.160 --> 00:12:59.410
whole percentage points out of the cost
structure and that's been passed on to

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:04.750
the customers in much lower prices. So a
very low produce prices and very low

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:08.020
center store prices and then we get...we
have Whole Foods market's kind of

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:12.380
prepared foods selection and our quality
standards are still in effect. So we

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:16.030
think this is a very successful format
that we're probably going to have

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:19.590
hundreds of these stores within
not that many years.

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:24.000
And will they compete against Whole Foods market or do they supplement it or how does that work?

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:28.470
There will be some competition of course,
there'll be some overlap in customers. But what

00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:33.190
we found so far is the cannibalization
has been less than we anticipated. That

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:37.460
we're getting a customer in that's more
price-sensitive that we were not getting

00:13:37.460 --> 00:13:42.450
into Whole Foods market stores. And so
the number of Trader Joes bags that

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:44.720
we've seen shopping in our stores is
unprecedented.

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:47.520
Is that kind of the direct competitor for the (crosstalk)

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:50.360
I mean in some ways 365 competes with

00:13:50.360 --> 00:13:55.320
Trader Joes. It competes with Sprouts
farmers markets. But of course the main

00:13:55.320 --> 00:13:59.910
competitors are the mainstream
supermarkets like Kroger and HEB and

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:05.890
Wegmans. But we now have a model...a
business model that allows us to be less

00:14:05.890 --> 00:14:11.820
expensive and not be under sold by
anyone. And that's a good place to be in

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:15.760
Speaking of cannibalization, let's talk
about the movie that you've helped

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:22.360
produce "At the Fork" which is done by the...
Lisa and John Papola...the great

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:31.110
documentary filmmakers. And it is about
John's kind of move towards veganism or

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:35.050
understanding of animal welfare in the
food production change.

00:14:35.050 --> 00:14:39.290
Well of course John has now become a vegan. John Papola and Lisa Versaci

00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:42.280
are married couple and they are...

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:52.200
Lisa is a vegetarian. John is a carnivore.
And the film starts out with at a Papola

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:57.830
family gathering where they're eating ribs.

00:14:57.840 --> 00:15:01.040
It's called..is it the ribfest? Or meat feast? (crosstalk) yeah.

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:09.040
And that shows that. And then Lisa expresses her disapproval of that and they begin this journey

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:14.320
into America basically to see how our animal foods are raised. And what's fascinating about the

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:20.320
film is the film itself is very
non-judgmental and lets the camera do

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:25.680
the talking and it lets every...so it goes
from some of the factory farm producers

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:31.500
all the way up to some smaller animal
ranchers who are treating their animals

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:35.240
surprisingly very, very well high. So
they're all different grades of welfare treatment.

00:15:35.240 --> 00:15:39.459
And each in a sense allows
each of the animal farmers or ranchers

00:15:39.459 --> 00:15:42.259
to give their own philosophy.

00:15:42.259 --> 00:15:44.259
I mean it's actually...really I found it fascinating

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:49.680
because it's a movie...it is about
a journey and there aren't villains here

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:54.630
or the farmers...the factory
farmers aren't treated as poor stupid

00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:59.050
people who only care about a buck. But
they do have constraints and they

00:15:59.050 --> 00:16:03.270
voice time and time again like I will treat my livestock anyway

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:05.290
that my customers ask me to.

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:11.639
It is fascinating because their
philosophies get articulated and so the

00:16:11.639 --> 00:16:17.721
film is remarkably fair. It's unlike some
films might come in with a a real biased

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:21.960
point of view and then set it...say if
Michael Moore was producing a documentary

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:24.720
he would he would come in with a bias and
then make sure the film comes out that way.

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:29.490
This was a discovery process and
they let these far- they're fair to all the

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:33.920
farmers. They all get a chance
to get their philosophical perspective.

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:38.400
Are there farmers...because I was taken by
the you know at various stages as they get closer to

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:43.040
a more kind of animal friendly
generally a higher-end price point and stuff like that.

00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:47.680
But all of the producers pretty much say some variation of I don't really

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:52.080
have a stake in how I
raise my...like what I'm doing is to serve the

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:56.000
customer and if the customer
wants something at a certain price

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:59.240
I'm going to do it in a more industrial
way or less industrial way.

00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:03.309
Are there people who are ideologically
committed to kind of factory farm

00:17:03.309 --> 00:17:09.280
processes or you know treating livestock
in the least thoughtful way?

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:14.400
I don't know if ideology is what I would say... the overwhelming driving thing has

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:20.160
always been in food production is how
can we get costs down? Just like Whole

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:23.760
Foods market with 365 is thinking
through our business model how can we

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:28.230
take cost out so we can get lower
prices to our customers? That same logic

00:17:28.240 --> 00:17:35.080
carries for...that's why capitalism
works. And here you have is one of the...

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:42.930
James McWilliams says...a professor of
history...says these decisions that moved

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:47.120
us towards the factory farm system were
hundreds, if not thousands of small

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:51.760
little innovations that took us in this
direction of taking costs out of the system.

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:58.839
And ultimately the lowest cost
system is one that has scale and which

00:17:58.839 --> 00:18:07.081
minimizes feed inputs and all the
other factors of production. And that

00:18:07.120 --> 00:18:14.320
tends to be by most dispassionate
viewers is pretty inhumane. Pretty cruel to the animals...

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:15.400
You are a vegan yourself?

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:16.440
Correct.

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:19.480
Can you briefly recount your conversion?

00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:24.200
A little over 13 years ago I was at an annual

00:18:24.200 --> 00:18:29.280
Whole Foods market annual meeting
for our stockholders in Santa Monica, CA.

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:35.120
We were being picketed by
animal rights activists and they were

00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:38.210
disrupting our meeting. I wasn't very happy
about that. I thought like they don't...

00:18:38.240 --> 00:18:41.630
they shouldn't even be here. This is
inappropriate. But I got into a discussion

00:18:41.630 --> 00:18:44.540
with the activists after the
meeting was over. In particular this one

00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:51.360
very tiny but passionate young woman
named Lauren Ornelas and we began an

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:55.091
email exchange and we were going back
and forth because for my attitude back

00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:57.821
then was I don't know why they're
picking on us. We're doing more for

00:18:57.840 --> 00:19:02.460
animals than anybody else. Why aren't they going after Safeway or Kroger for example.

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:07.850
And because...as they would say, "Well we
thought you would actually listen.

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:15.520
We didn't think they would." And I remember her telling me she said, "John, I can see

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:18.910
that you're actually a very idealistic man. You're not like I thought you would be

00:19:18.910 --> 00:19:22.450
I didn't think...you're not a typical
corporate CEO. But when it comes to

00:19:22.480 --> 00:19:28.240
animal welfare and the way animal
livestock animals are treated in the United States

00:19:28.240 --> 00:19:30.720
you are remarkably ignorant." And...

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:31.680
Was she right about that?

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:35.200
Yes. And you shouldn't be because you're
running a fairly large corporation and

00:19:35.240 --> 00:19:40.000
you sort of owe it to all of your
customers and employees to know better

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:44.720
about these things. So I was taken aback
and I realize you know she's probably

00:19:44.720 --> 00:19:47.690
right about that. I could be better
informed. So that summer...summer of

00:19:47.690 --> 00:19:53.140
2003 I read about a dozen books on
livestock animals in America.

00:19:53.140 --> 00:19:58.940
How it evolved, practices. And God about midway through after I read four or five books I thought

00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:04.600
you know she's really right. I don't know...I
didn't know anything and furthermore we

00:20:04.600 --> 00:20:06.600
gotta change and do something about it.

00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:10.850
By the time the summer was over I said,
"I'm not going to eat animals anymore."

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:15.500
I became a vegan and from
an ethical standpoint and then also decided

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:19.150
you know Whole Foods needs to do a lot
more here. So we invited the activists to

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:24.700
work with us as...on our team so to
speak and then bring in animal

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:29.730
scientists, bring in the farmers
themselves. Bring them together into

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:35.850
meetings and let's talk about how we can
develop more higher animal welfare

00:20:35.850 --> 00:20:39.950
systems that have higher...and so 
that's how I converted and that's what

00:20:39.950 --> 00:20:41.210
Whole Foods has been doing ever since.

00:20:41.210 --> 00:20:46.060
Is there a point where the you know
where you will from a kind of moral

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:50.640
perspective stop selling animal products
or like how do you square that

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:54.300
between you're treating the animals
better but you're still selling them.

00:20:54.320 --> 00:20:59.000
Is that because the customer wants them or where when does that...

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:00.600
You know it's funny. I get asked that question a lot.

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:04.250
And I suppose because I'm so closely
identified with the Whole Foods market

00:21:04.250 --> 00:21:08.080
brand and people for the most part
don't understand how corporations work

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:12.970
they think, quite incorrectly, that Whole
Foods market is like my company.

00:21:12.970 --> 00:21:17.310
That I'm like this all-powerful owner,
that can do whatever I want. But in

00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:21.389
fact, I founded the company, but we're a
very large corporation at this point and

00:21:21.389 --> 00:21:28.711
it...I always tell the story that let's
say I wanted to do that. I would not be

00:21:28.720 --> 00:21:32.560
able to do it. If I said, "You know what
guys? I really think we need to stop

00:21:32.560 --> 00:21:37.210
selling meat. That's just the right thing
to do. And so effective this date I'm

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:42.160
issuing a command as the as co-founder,
co-CEO that we're going to phase out of meat."

00:21:42.160 --> 00:21:45.880
Well everybody would think it was a joke. They would laugh and

00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:51.539
I'd say, "No, no. I'm very serious about
this. I have seen the light. We are going

00:21:51.539 --> 00:21:55.301
to stop selling meat at Whole Foods
market." And they would say, "John, you've

00:21:55.301 --> 00:21:58.880
got to be kidding. You know we can't do
that. It would completely ruin the business."

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:02.240
I say, "Nevertheless, it's the right thing to
do and by God we're going to do it."

00:22:02.240 --> 00:22:05.929
About that time of course the rest of the
executive team would be like, "What is

00:22:05.929 --> 00:22:10.121
happening? John is like going crazy." But
then if I persisted calls would go to the

00:22:10.160 --> 00:22:15.360
board of directors and say, "We got to do
something. He's about to wreck our business."

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:18.880
And I would get calls from the board and
they would say, "John, you know you can...

00:22:18.880 --> 00:22:22.520
you can't do this. You're going to have to
back off on this." And I'd say, "No, this is right

00:22:22.520 --> 00:22:26.480
thing to do by God I'm gonna do it." Well
if I continue to persist I would be

00:22:26.481 --> 00:22:30.890
removed as a CEO. That's just the reality
of the situation. That's the way business sort of works.

00:22:30.890 --> 00:22:35.531
And that's the right thing for them to do because Whole Foods market ultimately...

00:22:35.531 --> 00:22:40.200
ultimately has to serve its customers. And I'd like nothing

00:22:40.200 --> 00:22:44.630
better than the customers to
vote with their dollars for Whole Foods market

00:22:44.640 --> 00:22:48.510
to stop selling animal foods. But until that vote occurs

00:22:48.510 --> 00:22:51.720
we're going to continue to sell them
because that's what they want to buy.

00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:56.450
And that's part of the function of "At the
Fork", right? That...I mean you're...it's not a

00:22:56.480 --> 00:23:00.240
preachey movie. I mean it is didactic,
there's a message there, but it's

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:02.240
hopefully raising people's consciousness.

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:05.920
I really think that "At the Fork"... if it has a purpose...its to raise people's
consciousness

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:07.760
because the average American has

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:11.780
no idea how animals are
raised. They don't want to know. There's

00:23:11.780 --> 00:23:16.589
sort of massive cultural denial about it.
I actually think that a hundred years from now

00:23:16.589 --> 00:23:20.071
we'll look back on the factory farm
era with the same kind of ethical

00:23:20.080 --> 00:23:22.000
revulsion that we look back on

00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:28.390
say slavery. Which was obviously completely morally reprehensible by any standard.

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:33.590
And I think this will be seen in a
similar light. Although when I say that

00:23:33.600 --> 00:23:39.089
sometimes people are offended because
they don't...they see the moral

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:42.661
revulsion of slavery but they have not yet
opened their eyes to see the moral

00:23:42.661 --> 00:23:46.599
reversion what we're doing to factory
farm animals. So this film is an attempt to...

00:23:46.599 --> 00:23:52.891
it's PG-13. We're not showing the
most horrible stuff because otherwise

00:23:52.891 --> 00:23:54.770
people just walk out of the theater.

00:23:54.800 --> 00:23:59.390
We're giving it in a measured dose and
most importantly we're showing them that

00:23:59.390 --> 00:24:05.060
there are better alternatives. So if I
had my way when people finish watching

00:24:05.060 --> 00:24:08.610
that film they, they'd be faced with an
ethical dilemma which is I'm going to

00:24:08.640 --> 00:24:13.200
either I'm not going to eat meat anymore
or I'm only going to eat higher animal welfare meat.

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:16.640
And that would be the choice I'd like to see
people make.

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:19.680
Well the movie is "At the Fork" and we have been talking

00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:23.680
to Whole Foods co-founder and co-CEO John Mackey.

00:24:23.680 --> 00:24:25.660
Thanks so much for talking to us, John.

00:24:25.660 --> 00:24:26.560
Thanks, Nick.

00:24:26.560 --> 00:24:28.200
For Reason TV, I'm Nick Gillespie.

